--- DESCRIPTION ---
How Turnly tracks parallel timelines in Japan and North Korea over almost three decades.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Two Worlds, One Stage</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2"><em>The Great Wave</em> by Francis Turnly spans almost a quarter of a century &mdash; from 1979 to 2003 &mdash; across two nations that share a coastline but almost nothing else. The structural challenge Turnly sets himself is formidable: how do you tell a story in which two families live through the same event from opposite sides of a border, across two decades, without ever fully knowing the same facts? His answer is a rigorously disciplined <dfn class="term" data-def="A dramatic structure in which two or more separate storylines are presented in alternating scenes, often to draw contrasts or reveal connections.">cross-cutting</dfn> structure that forces the audience into the uncomfortable position of knowing more than any single character ever does.</p>

<p data-narration-id="n3">The play is structured in two acts. The first act establishes the catastrophic event at its centre &mdash; the abduction of Hanako from the coast of Japan by North Korean agents during a storm in 1979 &mdash; and then splits into its two parallel lines: Hanako in North Korea, and her family in Japan. The second act moves deeper into adulthood, into diplomacy and silence, and towards the Pyongyang Declaration of 2002, which acknowledged for the first time that such abductions had taken place. By choosing this two-act, twin-timeline structure, Turnly gives the audience a form that mirrors its content: separation, parallel endurance, and the impossibility of reunion that looks easy from the outside.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n4" data-revision-tip="Without looking, describe the two parallel storylines of The Great Wave and the years they span. Name one scene from each storyline.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The play spans 1979 to 2003 across two parallel storylines: Hanako in North Korea (abducted as a teenager) and her family in Japan (searching, grieving, advocating). The twin timelines converge emotionally but almost never physically.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n5">The Opening Beach Scene</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n6">The play opens in a storm. Two teenage sisters &mdash; Hanako and Reiko &mdash; are on the coast of Japan, arguing. Turnly&rsquo;s stage directions foreground the sea: its sound, its scale, its menace. This is the last time we will see both sisters together in the same present moment for the rest of the play. For a director, the staging of this scene is one of the most consequential choices in the whole production. Working in an <dfn class="term" data-def="A stage configuration where the audience sits on all four sides of the acting area, so performers are always seen from multiple angles.">in-the-round</dfn> configuration, the sea becomes a surround-sound presence rather than a painted flat: waves can come from all directions, making the audience feel the disorientation of the storm. In a more traditional <dfn class="term" data-def="A stage configuration where the audience sits in a single bank facing the acting area across the fourth wall, with an ornamental arch framing the stage opening.">proscenium arch</dfn> configuration, the beach might be signalled by a raked stage, a strip of projected surf at the back, and a soundscape of crashing water that gradually overwhelms the sisters&rsquo; voices.</p>

<p data-narration-id="n7">As a performer playing Reiko in this opening moment, the choices you make about posture and proximity signal the relationship before a word is spoken. Place Reiko upstage of Hanako and have her turn away as Hanako tries to speak &mdash; the physical distance communicates the argument&rsquo;s emotional temperature before either line is delivered. As a sound designer, you might begin with a low, continuous underscore of distant waves, then swell the volume as the sisters&rsquo; argument reaches its peak, so that the sea literally drowns their voices at the moment of separation &mdash; a design choice that carries the controlling metaphor of the title into the audience&rsquo;s ears.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>What is &lsquo;cross-cutting&rsquo; and why does Turnly use it?</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n8">Cross-cutting means the play shifts between its two storylines in alternating scenes rather than completing one before starting the other. In <em>The Great Wave</em>, Turnly cuts from Hanako&rsquo;s captivity in North Korea to the Hashimoto family in Japan and back again, sometimes within the same act. This structure creates dramatic irony &mdash; the audience knows Hanako is alive while her family does not &mdash; and it holds both truths simultaneously rather than letting the audience forget either one.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n9">For a director, these transitions need a clear <dfn class="term" data-def="A controlled change in light intensity, colour or area on stage, used to signal shifts in time, place or mood.">lighting state</dfn> change at each cross-cut. A common approach is a sudden blackout followed immediately by a new state &mdash; colder in North Korea, warmer in Japan &mdash; so the audience registers the geography shift through temperature and colour before any dialogue confirms it. The costume of the performers can reinforce this: subdued utility fabrics and muted earth tones in the DPRK sequences; softer domestic textures and layered clothing in the Japanese family sequences.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n10">Time Leaps and the Ageing Body</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n11">Because the play moves across more than two decades, the performers playing Hanako and her family must communicate ageing without realistic prosthetics. Turnly&rsquo;s stage directions indicate time leaps through lighting changes, sound shifts and costume alteration. A <dfn class="term" data-def="The positioning of actors in relation to each other and to the space, decided by the director and communicated through rehearsal.">blocking</dfn> strategy commonly used to communicate ageing is to give a character a progressively reduced range of movement as years accumulate &mdash; a youthful Hanako who crosses the stage in a few quick strides becomes, a decade later, a figure who moves in deliberate, contained steps, her energy disciplined by years of captivity. This is not naturalistic ageing &mdash; it is a theatrical shorthand that makes the passage of time legible in the performer&rsquo;s body.</p>

<p data-narration-id="n12">For the audience, this physical storytelling across time is more powerful than any chronological caption could be. When Etsuko, the mother, is seen in two scenes separated by a decade, a lighting designer might shift from a warmer, amber-tinted state in the earlier scene to a cooler, lower-intensity wash in the later one &mdash; signalling not just time but emotional weathering. A <dfn class="term" data-def="A metal plate with a cut-out shape that slots into a profile lantern to project a pattern of light onto the stage.">gobo</dfn> patterned to suggest window bars or a lattice might appear in the North Korean scenes to suggest confinement without building a cell.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n13" data-revision-tip="Name two staging techniques Turnly uses to signal time leaps. For each, describe one specific design choice a director might use to make the transition clear to the audience.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Turnly signals time leaps through lighting state changes, costume alterations and sound cues rather than captions or narration. This places the burden of storytelling on design rather than exposition, which gives the audience an active interpretive role.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n14">Epic Structure and Documentary Roots</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n15">The structure of <em>The Great Wave</em> belongs to the tradition of <dfn class="term" data-def="A form of theatre that presents factual events or testimonies on stage, drawing on historical sources, interviews or documentary evidence.">documentary theatre</dfn> &mdash; a tradition associated with the Tricycle Theatre (now the Kiln Theatre) in Kilburn, where the play premiered in 2018 under director Indhu Rubasingham before transferring to the National Theatre&rsquo;s Dorfman stage. Documentary theatre typically presents itself as evidence rather than fiction; even though <em>The Great Wave</em> has invented characters, its structural discipline &mdash; the dual timeline, the diplomatic scenes, the precision of its historical markers (1979, 1983, 2002, 2003) &mdash; signals to the audience that these events are rooted in documented fact.</p>

<p data-narration-id="n16">This epic span puts the play in company with Brechtian <dfn class="term" data-def="A theatrical style, developed by Bertolt Brecht, that uses a sequence of distinct episodes rather than a continuous single storyline to present a political or social argument.">epic theatre</dfn> structure, in which scenes function as separate episodes each carrying their own meaning, rather than as links in a single causal chain. Brecht believed that episodic structure prevented the audience from becoming lost in emotional identification; they could stand back and witness the political argument being made. In <em>The Great Wave</em>, the cutting between Japan and North Korea achieves a similar effect: the audience cannot fully surrender to either storyline without being reminded that another, equally real world is running parallel to it.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Common misconception: is this play just about a Japanese family?</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n17">A common student error is to describe the play as a family drama about Japanese grief and treat the North Korean sequences as background. This misreads the structure entirely. Turnly gives roughly equal stage time to both sides; the North Korean sequences are not backdrop but a parallel central story. The examiner&rsquo;s note on this is consistent: students who analyse only the Japanese family&rsquo;s storyline miss the structural argument the play is making. The cross-cutting is the meaning. What the parallel structure demands of the audience is the emotional labour of holding both experiences at once &mdash; which is precisely what the abduction has denied the characters themselves.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n19">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n20">The play uses a disciplined cross-cutting structure across two parallel timelines (Japan and North Korea, 1979&ndash;2003), creating dramatic irony by giving the audience more knowledge than any single character possesses.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n21">Time leaps are communicated through lighting state changes, soundscape shifts and costume alterations rather than narration, placing the burden of structural storytelling on design.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n22">The play&rsquo;s epic, episodic structure draws on documentary theatre and Brechtian traditions; its dual timeline is not just a narrative device but the central political argument of the whole play.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n18">For extended-response questions on the structure of <em>The Great Wave</em>, the most common error is writing a scene-by-scene plot summary rather than analysing the structural choices Turnly makes and how they communicate meaning to the audience. The question will ask you to discuss how a director would stage a particular moment or how the playwright uses structure to achieve an effect &mdash; both require you to be concrete. Name the design choice (lighting state change, soundscape swell, blocking of ageing), say when it happens (the opening beach storm; the first interrogation; the diplomatic scene), and say what effect it creates for the audience. For 20-mark extended-response questions, spread your answer across at least two design disciplines (lighting and sound, for example) plus performer choices. Students who only discuss one element rarely reach the highest marks.</p>

