=== L7 Performance Interpretation: Hanako Across Twenty Years ===
id: 7c29c175-0bb6-4c4e-90a0-391f6e49aa3c
slug: performance-interpretation-hanako-across-twenty-years
status: live tier:both updated:2026-08-19T11:13:09.126759+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 22

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Vocal and physical choices for Hanako, Reiko and Etsuko that age them across two decades.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">The Challenge of Playing Time</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">A performer playing Hanako in <em>The Great Wave</em> faces one of the most demanding tasks in any of the nine set plays: she must show a journey from a defiant 17-year-old arguing on a storm-swept beach in 1979 to a woman in her late thirties who has survived captivity, forced re-education, and the slow erosion of her original self. She does this without the aid of sophisticated prosthetics or years of naturalistic preparation &mdash; she does it through the precision of vocal and physical choices, scene by scene, across the play&rsquo;s 24-year arc.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n3">This is not simply about playing &ldquo;older.&rdquo; Playing older is a surface effect (slowed movement, deeper voice). What the play requires is something more specific: tracking how Hanako&rsquo;s relationship to <dfn class="term" data-def="The physical and spatial relationship between two performers on stage, used to communicate status, emotional connection, or threat.">proxemics</dfn>, to space, to silence, changes as she learns to survive inside a system designed to contain her. The body that moves freely and claims space on the beach is not the same body ten years into captivity. The difference is not just age &mdash; it is <em>accommodation</em>.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n4" data-revision-tip="Cover this and describe two specific physical changes a performer might use to show Hanako at 17 on the beach versus Hanako ten years into captivity. Be precise: name the specific physical choice.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Playing Hanako across time requires a performer to track three layers simultaneously: <strong>age</strong> (physical changes in posture, pace, breath); <strong>accommodation</strong> (how the body learns to move within a system of surveillance); and <strong>internal resistance</strong> (the private moments where the self underneath the performance emerges). All three must be visible at different moments of the play.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n5">Hanako: Physical Containment and Release</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n6">In the beach scene &mdash; Hanako&rsquo;s first appearance and her last moment of freedom &mdash; a performer can play her in full ownership of her body: wide stance, <dfn class="term" data-def="The planned pattern of movement and positioning of performers on stage, created by the director to create meaning and guide the audience&rsquo;s eye.">blocking</dfn> that cuts across the stage diagonally, gestures that reach outward. The argument with Reiko in the storm can use full vocal range &mdash; high pitch on challenge, fast pace as the argument escalates, volume that competes with the implied sound of the wind. This is a young woman who has not yet learned that her body can be owned by someone else.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n7">The contrast in the first captivity scenes should be stark. Now, the performer might begin reducing the size of every gesture: arms held closer to the body, movements that do not claim more space than strictly necessary, eye-contact that is carefully managed &mdash; not downcast (that would signal defeat too early) but controlled, the eye-contact of someone learning when it is safe to look and when it is not. The voice becomes more <dfn class="term" data-def="A vocal quality where the performer uses a narrow range of pitch and volume, suggesting suppression, control or a deliberate concealment of feeling.">contained</dfn>: lower register, measured pace, pauses that are not emotional hesitation but <em>strategic</em> hesitation &mdash; calculating what to say rather than feeling unable to say it.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n8">The &ldquo;release moments&rdquo; &mdash; private scenes where Hanako is not under official observation &mdash; give the performer the opportunity to show what is still alive underneath the accommodation. A sudden small physical release (letting the hands open, a brief change in spine alignment) can communicate volumes. These moments should be very short &mdash; the accommodation has become second nature, the release is a flash &mdash; and they function as a kind of dramatic punctuation, reminding the audience that Hanako is not the role the state has written for her.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>The interrogation scenes: stillness as a performance choice</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n9">The interrogation scenes are some of the play&rsquo;s most demanding. Hanako is being asked to confirm or deny an identity &mdash; to accept a new name, a new history. A performer who plays these scenes by trying to &ldquo;look scared&rdquo; misses the point entirely. The more interesting and more truthful choice is <dfn class="term" data-def="A deliberate performance choice to remain physically motionless at a significant moment, using stillness itself to create tension or communicate internal state.">stillness</dfn>: absolute stillness, placed centre stage under a harsh overhead light, with the minimum of facial expression. This choice tells the audience that Hanako is conserving herself &mdash; spending as little energy as possible, giving as little as possible, preserving something inside that the system cannot reach.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n10">Vocally, the same principle applies. A flat, even delivery in the interrogation scenes &mdash; not dead, but carefully neutral &mdash; communicates the same conservation of self. The voice is not the character&rsquo;s private voice; it is the voice produced for the officials. The distinction between this voice and the rare private voice (warmer, perhaps slightly faster) is something the audience begins to read as the play goes on, so that a shift in vocal register becomes a signal of private self re-emerging.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n11">Reiko: Grief That Does Not Resolve</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n12">The performer playing Reiko faces a different temporal challenge: not the gradual accommodation of captivity but the gradual hardening of unresolved grief into something that looks like certainty. Reiko spends the play refusing to accept that Hanako is dead. In the early scenes this refusal is raw &mdash; it can be played with a vocal tension that sits high in the chest, a physical restlessness, hands that cannot settle. In the middle scenes, the grief has been lived with long enough that it has taken on structure &mdash; it has become a project, an advocacy position. The <dfn class="term" data-def="The management of physical space between performer and other cast members, which changes to show shifts in relationship, power, or emotional state.">proxemic patterns</dfn> change: Reiko moves toward people, demanding to be taken seriously, whereas early-grief Reiko might move away, closing off.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n13">For a performer, the key is never to play the same version of Reiko twice. Each scene must show the accumulation of years. A simple physical marker &mdash; the way she holds herself when addressing officials, a very slight brittleness in the voice that was not there at the beginning &mdash; communicates the weight of two decades without requiring the performer to play every moment at full emotional intensity.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n14">Etsuko: Resignation That Is Not Defeat</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n15">The mother, Etsuko, ages the most visibly of the three central women across the play&rsquo;s timeline. But the danger in playing her is to reduce her ageing to physical signifiers only (slower movement, quieter voice) without tracking her interior journey. Etsuko&rsquo;s trajectory is from active maternal grief to something that examiners often describe as &ldquo;resignation&rdquo; &mdash; but a performer who plays resignation as a collapse misses the point. Etsuko&rsquo;s silence in the later scenes is not weakness; it is the outcome of having waited so long that the waiting has become her identity. A performer might express this through very deliberate, economical physicality &mdash; each movement purposeful, nothing wasted &mdash; and a vocal quality that is low and steady, suggesting not defeat but endurance.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n16" data-revision-tip="Without looking, describe one vocal choice and one physical choice for Reiko in her early grief scenes and explain how each communicates her emotional state to the audience.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>All three central performers must show <strong>change over time</strong>: this is the play&rsquo;s deepest requirement. Not a single fixed characterisation but a performance in which the audience can feel the weight of 24 years accumulating in the body and voice. Examiners reward answers that track <em>specific</em> changes &mdash; not just &ldquo;she becomes sadder&rdquo; but &ldquo;in the early scenes her voice is in the upper register with rapid pace; by the final act the pace is slower and the pitch lower, suggesting the energy of grief has become the quiet weight of endurance.&rdquo;</p>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Misconception: playing Hanako &lsquo;sad&rsquo; across twenty years</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n17">A very common performance-interpretation error is to reduce Hanako&rsquo;s 24-year journey to a single emotional note: sadness, or alternatively resilience. Both are too simple. Hanako&rsquo;s journey includes defiance, survival, accommodation, moments of real connection (with Jung Sun), moments of private resistance, and eventually the confirmation that her family has known she was alive &mdash; a discovery that is not straightforwardly joyful. A performer who has made a specific, evolving set of physical and vocal choices throughout the play will be able to land that final complexity in a way that a performer playing a single emotion cannot. &ldquo;I would play her as sad&rdquo; scores at Developing. &ldquo;In the teaching scenes I would allow a small warmth to enter the voice when addressing Jung Sun &mdash; a slightly raised pitch, a fractionally faster pace &mdash; as the only moment where the accommodation shows a crack in its surface&rdquo; scores at Mastering.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n19">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n20">Playing Hanako requires tracking three layers across 24 years: ageing (posture, pace, breath), accommodation (learning to contain the body within a system of surveillance), and internal resistance (the release moments that show the self underneath). Each layer needs specific, changing physical and vocal choices scene by scene.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n21">Stillness and vocal neutrality in the interrogation scenes communicate Hanako&rsquo;s strategy of self-preservation &mdash; not defeat. The contrast between her contained official voice and her warmer private voice becomes a signal the audience learns to read over the course of the play.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n22">Reiko and Etsuko must also show change over time: Reiko&rsquo;s grief hardening into advocacy and Etsuko&rsquo;s grief becoming the endurance of long waiting. Neither character can be played as a single fixed emotion &mdash; the accumulation of years must be legible in the body and voice.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n18">For &ldquo;How would you (as a performer) interpret&rdquo; questions (8 marks), the most penalised error is describing an emotional state without translating it into specific vocal and physical choices. &ldquo;I would play Hanako as frightened&rdquo; earns almost nothing. You must specify: <strong>the vocal element</strong> (pitch &mdash; low to signal suppression; pace &mdash; measured to signal strategic control; volume &mdash; quiet but not shaky) AND <strong>the physical element</strong> (stillness, proxemics, gesture size, posture alignment) AND <strong>the named moment</strong> (in the first interrogation; in the beach scene; in the teaching scene with Jung Sun). Every 8-mark answer needs at least one named vocal choice, one named physical choice, and one named moment. For 20-mark extended staging responses, track how the performer&rsquo;s choices change across the play&rsquo;s timeline &mdash; examiners value answers that show awareness of how a performance must evolve, not just describe a single moment.</p>

--- PRACTICE_QUESTIONS ---
