  "text": "As a performer playing Hanako, explain how you would use vocal and physical choices to communicate her internal state in the scene when she first confronts her new identity in the North Korean compound. Refer to at least one vocal and one physical choice and explain the effect on the audience.",
  "type": "8 marks — Interpret as Performer",
  "marks": "Mastering: the response specifies concrete vocal choices (e.g. flat, even delivery at low volume — not dead but carefully neutral; measured pace with strategic pauses; a slight steadying of breath before each answer) AND specific physical choices (absolute stillness at centre stage; arms at sides, not held defensively; eye contact that is controlled rather than downcast — looking when safe, not when unsafe). The choices are anchored to the named scene (first interrogation) and the effect on the audience is stated: the audience reads Hanako’s stillness and neutrality as a strategy of self-preservation, understanding that she is spending as little of herself as possible. There is awareness of what this choice reveals about character.\n\nSecure: at least one specific vocal and one specific physical choice; named moment; effect on audience present.\n\nDeveloping: describes how Hanako is feeling (“scared,” “trying to be brave”) without translating feeling into specific vocal or physical choices.\n\nEmerging: says “I would act scared” or similar; no specific choices; no named moment."
 },
 {
  "text": "Analyse Turnly's intentions in showing the physical and vocal transformation of Hanako across the play's 24-year timeline. What does he want the audience to understand about the effect of captivity on an individual?",
  "type": "12 marks — Analyse Intentions",
  "marks": "Mastering: analyses Turnly’s intention to make captivity physically legible — not as melodrama but as a gradual, specific process of accommodation. The response links the performance arc (from the open, claiming physicality of the beach scene to the contained economy of the compound years) to Turnly’s political argument: what the DPRK abductions actually did to real people was not just imprison them but require them to perform a different self so thoroughly that the original self could only appear in private flashes. The analysis shows awareness of the play’s documentary roots and the ethical weight of staging a real person’s experience. Named moments are used (beach scene, first interrogation, teaching scenes, closing scenes).\n\nSecure: identifies the transformation and links it to Turnly’s themes of identity erasure; generally accurate; at least one named moment.\n\nDeveloping: describes the changes in Hanako without analysing what Turnly intends the audience to understand; effect is implied.\n\nEmerging: retells plot events without analysis of playwright’s intention."
 },
 {
  "text": "You are directing The Great Wave. Discuss how you would work with the performers playing Hanako, Reiko and Etsuko to show the passage of twenty-four years in their vocal and physical choices. Refer to specific performance choices for each character and their effect on the audience.",
  "type": "20 marks — Extended Staging Response",
  "marks": "Mastering: a specific, differentiated performance brief for all three characters. Hanako: tracks accommodation and private resistance through changing proxemics, gesture size, vocal register, and the contrast between official and private voices across named scenes. Reiko: tracks grief hardening into advocacy — early scenes with high vocal tension and physical restlessness; later scenes with a brittle steadiness and forward-moving proxemics; vocal change from raw grief to controlled insistence. Etsuko: tracks the endurance of waiting — economical physicality, each movement deliberate; low, steady vocal quality that is endurance not defeat; subtle make-up and physical marks of ageing supporting but not replacing the performer’s choices. All choices are specific, named to moments, and linked to audience effect. The response shows directorial intention: these three transformations are the play’s argument about what state violence costs individual human lives.\n\nSecure: at least two characters addressed with specific choices; generally concrete; effect on audience present.\n\nDeveloping: general suggestions (“she gets older and sadder”) without specific vocal and physical choices; one or two characters addressed.\n\nEmerging: describes what the characters go through rather than how a performer would communicate it; no specific choices."
 }
