 {
  "text": "As a performer playing Kum-Chol, the North Korean officer who oversees Hanako's captivity, explain how you would use vocal and physical choices to show that he is both complicit in the state system and recognisably human. Refer to at least one vocal and one physical choice and explain the effect on the audience.",
  "type": "8 marks — Interpret as Performer",
  "marks": "Mastering: the response holds both dimensions simultaneously. Physical choices that signal systemic authority (upright bearing, formal proxemics, a gestus of institutional belonging — e.g. a particular way of standing in a doorway that signals ownership of the space) alongside physical choices that reveal humanity (a moment of stillness when listening to Hanako that suggests something other than pure official engagement; a fractionally longer pause before an order). Vocal choices similarly: a formal, even register for official transactions, but a shift in tone or pace in private exchanges. The response anchors these to named moments and explains the effect on the audience: they are held in a dual response, understanding him as a perpetrator and as a person, which is the play’s moral complexity.\n\nSecure: at least one specific vocal and one physical choice; named moment; effect on audience present; some engagement with the dual character.\n\nDeveloping: describes what Kum-Chol is feeling without translating into specific choices; or plays only one dimension (either evil or sympathetic, not both).\n\nEmerging: generic emotional description; no specific choices; no named moment."
 },
 {
  "text": "As a lighting or sound designer, explain how you could use design choices to reinforce the Brechtian political frame of The Great Wave. Refer to at least one specific design choice and its effect on the audience.",
  "type": "8 marks — Interpret as Designer",
  "marks": "Mastering: the response proposes design choices that function as Brechtian devices — projected titles (dates, number of abductees, location) in a plain sans-serif typeface on the cyclorama at key transitions (interrupting the naturalistic fiction to insist on documentary reality); or a cold, high-contrast lighting state for the official scenes that makes them feel less naturalistic than the family scenes (a design “estrangement”); or a sound design that introduces institutional broadcast sound without the emotional preparation a naturalistic production would use (no gradual fade-in, just the sound arriving — a Brechtian shock cut). Every choice is named specifically (e.g. projected white sans-serif text at 80% opacity on the cyclorama; or a sudden cut to tinny propaganda audio at mid-volume from a single downstage speaker) and the effect on the audience is stated: the audience is reminded that this is a documented event, not a private story.\n\nSecure: at least two specific design choices; at least one linked to Brechtian technique; effect on audience present.\n\nDeveloping: general design ideas (“I would use cold lighting”) without linking to Brechtian technique or naming specific elements.\n\nEmerging: describes the desired atmosphere without specific design choices; no link to practitioner."
 },
 {
  "text": "You are directing The Great Wave. Discuss how you would apply both Brechtian techniques and Stanislavski's approach to different moments of the production to serve the play's dual purpose: political argument and emotional truth. Refer to specific moments of the play and specific staging choices.",
  "type": "20 marks — Extended Staging Response",
  "marks": "Mastering: a clear directorial vision is articulated that uses both practitioners purposefully. Brechtian choices: direct address at the official announcement scenes (audience positioned as jury); gestus for the officials and Kum-Chol (making their social role legible); projected title cards at scene transitions (insisting on documentary reality); a design language for the North Korean compound that is slightly less naturalistic than the Japan scenes (harder edges, less texture, more institutional geometry). Stanislavskian choices: detailed given circumstances for Hanako’s arc (what she has lost, what she still has, what she wants from each encounter); specific vocal and physical choices for the teaching scenes with Jung Sun (warmth, however provisional); the emotional truth of Etsuko’s waiting given full interior life. The response explains why each practitioner is appropriate to which type of scene and shows awareness that the two approaches reinforce rather than contradict each other. Named moments throughout. Audience effect throughout.\n\nSecure: both practitioners addressed with at least one specific application each; named moments; effect on audience present.\n\nDeveloping: one practitioner addressed in detail, the other mentioned but not applied; general choices.\n\nEmerging: describes what the practitioners believed without applying their techniques to the play; no named moments."
 }
]

