+    "field": "content_html",
+    "find": "<p data-narration-id=\"n10\">One of Turnly&rsquo;s most striking dramatic methods is the use of language as a marker of identity and coercion. In the North Korean scenes, English stands in for Japanese (the language Hanako speaks at home and later teaches) and stylised Korean signals that she is now living inside a system that wants to erase who she is. This <dfn class=\"term\" data-def=\"Moving between two languages or registers within a scene or even a single exchange, used here to signal identity, power, and the pressure to conform.\">code-switching</dfn> is a form of subtext: every time Hanako speaks in the language she has been forced to learn, the audience feels the cost of her survival.</p>",
